WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.462 --> 00:00:02.636
(city noises)

00:00:02.636 --> 00:00:05.219
(Jersey club beat)

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.330
- That kick pattern.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.219
Yeah, you know the one I'm talking about.

00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:16.802
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.330
That's a staple of the Jersey Club sound,

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.400
a sound that's gone viral
across YouTube and TikTok,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.510
making the whole world shake their hips.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.640
You've probably heard
the Jersey Club remixes

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.204
that have gone viral online.

00:00:29.204 --> 00:00:31.787
(upbeat music)

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.150
Or maybe you've heard
Jersey Club's influence

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.154
in top 40 hits like Level Up by Ciara.

00:00:40.154 --> 00:00:40.987
♪ Level up ♪

00:00:40.987 --> 00:00:42.191
♪ Level up ♪

00:00:42.191 --> 00:00:43.890
♪ Level up, level up, level up ♪

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.980
If you've been on TikTok, then
you definitely know this song

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.535
by Jersey native, Cookiee Kawaii.

00:00:48.535 --> 00:00:49.936
♪ Throw it back, throw it back ♪

00:00:49.936 --> 00:00:51.910
♪ Back, back, throw it back ♪

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.870
This sound originates from the Black DJs

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.750
of the Newark club scene,
and even though online data

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.150
shows that more women are
listening to Jersey Club than men,

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.720
the music production has been
historically male-dominated.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:04.940
Recently, however,

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.840
there's been a rise in women producers

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.550
making club music.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.190
But before we get into that story,

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.080
let's rewind a bit and talk about

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.680
where Jersey Club came from.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.590
Sound Field went to Newark, New Jersey,

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.290
to meet the dancers and
producers who make this music

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:21.803
and to find out what makes
this sound so contagious.

00:01:21.803 --> 00:01:25.145
(upbeat music)

00:01:25.145 --> 00:01:27.253
♪ I'm gonna leave it on the dance floor ♪

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.920
(crowd yelling)

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.440
So where did Jersey club come from?

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:34.273
On the surface,

00:01:34.273 --> 00:01:37.200
it's a hybrid of the
original Newark club sound

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.270
and the Chicago house sound.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.510
The Jersey sound was birthed in the 80s

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.300
at the legendary Zanzibar nightclub

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.700
in Newark, New Jersey

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.290
by musical icon, Tony Humphries.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:49.810
Humphries' music blended electronic beats

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.280
with soulful melodies,

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.473
becoming a staple in the nightclub scene.

00:01:53.473 --> 00:01:56.056
(upbeat music)

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.360
But it wasn't until the early 2000s

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.230
that it became the Jersey club
sound you recognize today.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.880
- It came a time where

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.940
the Chicago records stopped coming.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:11.830
So I was like, yo, I need something fresh.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:12.745
- DJ Tameil,

00:02:12.745 --> 00:02:15.840
a pioneer in the club
scene would travel to

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.110
Music Liberated, a hotspot
for local DJs in Baltimore.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:21.660
- We constantly went and bought vinyls.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.657
So I would just read all the
credits, all of the names,

00:02:24.657 --> 00:02:25.757
you know, the numbers,

00:02:25.757 --> 00:02:27.460
I called the number on it.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.530
The first guy I got in
touch with was this guy

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:33.800
named Bernie, rest in peace to Bernie.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.120
But he was like one of the
biggest labels down there.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.760
They would take all of
the B-more producers,

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.152
artists, and this and that.

00:02:41.152 --> 00:02:42.890
And you know, he'll put
their records out for him

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:44.930
and he owned his own record store.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.390
So, you know, when I would call him

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:48.510
he would just introduce
me to different people.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.760
- It was there that he met Baltimore DJs,

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.330
like DJ Technics and Rod Lee,

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:55.680
who gave him the blessing
to take the Baltimore style

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:56.715
back to Newark.

00:02:56.715 --> 00:02:59.298
(upbeat music)

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.190
I would just roam around here,

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.260
find everybody,

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:05.093
kick it with them.

00:03:05.093 --> 00:03:06.730
You know, we produced tracks together.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.970
We had no idea that it was
going to turn into this

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:09.803
right here.

00:03:09.803 --> 00:03:11.300
- It was this blending of styles

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.730
that gave birth to brick city club music,

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.360
which quickly became popular
across the state of New Jersey.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:17.430
Listen to the kick pattern

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.923
in Teena Marie Theme by DJ Tameil.

00:03:20.923 --> 00:03:23.506
(upbeat music)

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.160
After Tameil,

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.590
more and more Jersey DJs began
making their own club hits,

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.540
and gaining millions of plays online.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:36.518
DJs like Slink, Montana, and Lilman

00:03:36.518 --> 00:03:39.101
(upbeat music)

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.689
Then in 2009,

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:47.700
women began to move from the
dance floor to the DJ booth.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.450
That's when UNIIQU3 hit the scene.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.320
This is UNIIQU3.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.350
The queen of Jersey club,

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:01.040
a producer, artist, and DJ

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.080
who's propelling this 135 BPM genre

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.730
into a global phenomenon.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:07.480
(upbeat music)

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.315
♪ Everybody say woo woo ♪

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:11.410
♪ Woo woo ♪

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:12.960
- So in your own words

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.700
how would you describe Jersey club music?

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.844
- Jersey club music is
your morning coffee,

00:04:18.844 --> 00:04:20.800
it's your afternoon red bull,

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:22.940
it's your late night espresso shot,

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.760
it's rambunctious in
your face party music.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.030
- And what was it like for women

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:30.910
in the club scene when you got started?

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:34.500
- Right. Well, when I came up,
it was super male dominated.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.500
We had a couple of women,
come and MC on tracks,

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.940
like Ms. Porsh.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:43.660
She was one of the first
women I heard on a club track,

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.270
and I was just like, yo, this is hot.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.530
We need more of this.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:48.022
We need girls DJing too.

00:04:48.022 --> 00:04:50.620
I'm a part of the second
generation of club music.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.260
There wasn't really a
blueprint for me to look up to.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:56.490
It was very, just dude behind the DJ booth

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:57.830
and all the girls would be,

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:58.840
you know, dancing,

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.900
shaking it to the left and the right.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.560
Not a lot of female representation

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:04.023
other than on the dance floor.

00:05:04.023 --> 00:05:06.630
(upbeat music)

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.460
Can you let me know who were some people

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.406
in the Jersey club world that
you look up to right now?

00:05:12.406 --> 00:05:14.392
Who really inspires you?

00:05:14.392 --> 00:05:16.420
- Queen and Kia.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:17.420
- Top two.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.450
- Yeah. And I really don't
know no other dancers.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:21.530
Male dancers, that's all I know.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:22.740
And those are the only two females.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.880
- So, and how come, you know,

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:26.613
you think that there's not enough girls

00:05:26.613 --> 00:05:29.593
in the Jersey club culture representing.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.900
- They don't get enough
opportunities, in my opinion.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:34.733
- Right.

00:05:34.733 --> 00:05:36.770
- They don't get to go out
there and show their talent

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:38.560
like they want to, in my opinion.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:39.748
- Not like the boys do.

00:05:39.748 --> 00:05:42.010
- Yeah, the boys y'all out there.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:43.280
Thinking they're all that.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.979
And you know, some girls,

00:05:44.979 --> 00:05:47.920
men don't know, some
girls can go out there.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.221
And just steal the spotlight, just saying.

00:05:50.221 --> 00:05:52.804
(upbeat music)

00:05:57.910 --> 00:05:59.433
- I just feel like females,

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.625
maybe they don't see other UNIIQU3s.

00:06:02.625 --> 00:06:03.690
Like who do you think
you have to look up to

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.060
To be like, I can do it.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:07.594
But now that there's
somebody like Uniique3

00:06:07.594 --> 00:06:09.690
and other female DJs out here doing it.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.470
Maybe they can be like, you know what?

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.470
I don't have to be shaking my
booty in front of the camera.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.150
Maybe I can be behind the
scenes, making the music

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:16.513
that's making people shake their booties.

00:06:16.513 --> 00:06:19.096
(upbeat music)

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:22.460
- There are more women now taking over.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.760
Basically when it comes
to, like I say, UNIIQU3,

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.240
she's one of the main girls.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.073
Cookiees out here.

00:06:28.073 --> 00:06:29.250
She's doing her thing.

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:32.100
It's just before there
were all men, that's it.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.703
It's all men scratching.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.510
And now you just feel
way more comfortable.

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:40.470
(crowd talking)

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:42.550
- We have something so unique in our state

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:43.610
and within Jersey club,

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.190
like there's no way that it
shouldn't be on the big screen

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:47.941
or mainstream or anywhere like.

00:06:47.941 --> 00:06:49.370
UNIIQU3 done started it,

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:50.680
Slink done started it.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:52.310
Everybody done just took it further.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:54.884
So it was like the best part about it is

00:06:54.884 --> 00:06:55.717
the fact that a female

00:06:55.717 --> 00:06:58.140
is even taking it even further
on top of other females.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.747
So that's just the dopest part about it.

00:06:59.747 --> 00:07:01.743
And it deserves to go everywhere.

00:07:01.743 --> 00:07:04.102
Everybody listening, tune in
on SoundCloud to DJ UNIIQU3.

00:07:04.102 --> 00:07:05.414
and Cookiee Kawaii,

00:07:05.414 --> 00:07:06.953
everybody! So.

00:07:06.953 --> 00:07:08.260
(laughing)

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:10.470
- In Jersey, the ecosystem is composed of

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:12.170
four working parts.

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:15.853
Music, dance, business, and community.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.270
So what exactly makes a Jersey club song,

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:22.000
a Jersey club song?

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.392
You remember that kick pattern?

00:07:23.392 --> 00:07:25.809
(beat music)

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:28.870
This rhythm is an essential ingredient

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:30.050
in the Jersey club sound,

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.710
and it was inspired by Baltimore club.

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:34.610
- Oh, all of that stems
from, I don't know,

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.470
can I say that on here?

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:40.484
Okay. Well it was a
record called Dikkontrol.

00:07:40.484 --> 00:07:43.067
(upbeat music)

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:47.660
My partner Technics, which, you know,

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.120
I highly look up to just,

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:51.470
I got a lot of respect for him,

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.550
like how people got respect for me.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:55.659
And he blew my mind when he told me.

00:07:55.659 --> 00:08:00.659
He was in a DMZ DJ battle in 1993

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:02.320
and was juggling records

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.740
back and forth and did that
beat on the turntables.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:05.720
They went back to the studio

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.920
and recorded it and it became a club beat.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:10.070
But that one record we stuck to,

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:11.860
and we just took that kick pattern

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:12.970
and just kept going with it.

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:16.200
You know? So we just built from there.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.420
- Another thing that
Jersey club is famous for

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:20.160
is their use of sampling.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.690
- You know, anything we
can sample, we will use it.

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:24.430
And that's what Jersey club is.

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:28.060
I mean, Jersey club, B-more
club is sampled bass.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:28.940
The bed squeaks,

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:30.410
which everybody thinks is a bed squeak.

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:32.180
Lil John explained that,

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:33.920
which was mind blowing for us

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:36.304
because we always thought
it was a bed squeak.

00:08:36.304 --> 00:08:37.815
It's not a bed squeak.

00:08:37.815 --> 00:08:40.398
(upbeat music)

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:44.410
Lil John explained that
it's a rocking chair.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:46.060
But it sounds like a bed spring.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.297
But you know, we ran with it.

00:08:47.297 --> 00:08:49.128
It's still a bed spring to us.

00:08:49.128 --> 00:08:50.239
(upbeat music)

00:08:50.239 --> 00:08:53.140
♪ Give it to me, give it
to me, give it to me ♪

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.500
- As the music continues to evolve,

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.880
its sound has become more aggressive.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:08:59.340
Today, club tracks

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:00.940
switch up their four, four beat pattern

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:02.523
every eight to 16 bars.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:05.130
Then there's the vocals,

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.690
usually chopped up
samples of top 40 tracks,

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:09.360
along with column responsible pools

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:12.820
from independent artists
or the DJs themselves.

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:14.810
- The way that we chop up our vocals

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:17.120
are so clever because
at the end of the day,

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:19.890
it all has to lead to
making somebody dance.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.907
I just usually try to
find the best cadence.

00:09:21.907 --> 00:09:22.952
♪ Hands up, ♪

00:09:22.952 --> 00:09:24.290
♪ I'm gonna do my thing. ♪

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:25.580
Yeah. It came from a freestyle,

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:27.100
so I think I was just like,

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:28.980
spitting in my mic saying different stuff,

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.080
getting warmed up.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.400
And I was like, man,
this kind of sounds dope.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:33.985
♪ Back let me get it on the dance floor ♪

00:09:33.985 --> 00:09:35.710
♪ Stand back i'mma leave
it on the dance floor ♪

00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:40.620
♪ Stand back, stand back, stand back ♪

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:44.170
- Another important part of
Jersey club culture is remixing.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:46.380
And Jersey producers have
even helped other artists

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:48.710
Go viral through their club remixes.

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:52.490
- We find these records and
we make them cross over.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:56.740
So a drill trap record
that you wouldn't hear

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:58.900
at a certain demographic party,

00:09:58.900 --> 00:09:59.830
you could play it now,

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:02.050
because what we do is we transform it

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:05.047
into something that's
relatable to a wider audience.

00:10:05.047 --> 00:10:07.630
(upbeat music)

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:12.100
- But it's not just remixes,

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:13.510
Jersey Club Producers are also

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:15.340
putting out original dance music

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:18.190
that's played at clubs and
festivals around the world.

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:21.630
- Even though Jersey club
is known for its remixes

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:25.210
We have a plethora of
original Jersey club content

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:27.500
from you know, even UNIIQU3, you know,

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:29.260
being one of the curator
for the first ever

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:31.110
Spotify Jersey club playlist.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:34.214
There's a lot of original
content on that playlist.

00:10:34.214 --> 00:10:36.797
(upbeat music)

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:44.470
- There's only one rule
at Jersey club parties,

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:45.710
and that rule is simple.

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:47.553
Everyone must move to the beat.

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:53.440
There are even step-by-step
instructions in the music.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:56.020
So there's no excuse to be standing still.

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:58.600
The use of call and
response dance instructions,

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:00.810
and the evolution of the Jersey club sound

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.470
naturally gave weight to organize dances.

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:05.800
- So I, so if you want to
talk Jersey club, right?

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:07.270
A lot of times we're talking DJs,

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.640
talking producers, we're
talking the artists, right?

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.878
But we don't exist

00:11:11.878 --> 00:11:14.890
without the cornerstone of
what Jersey club was made for,

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:16.220
which is the dancer, right?

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:18.390
It's the reason why
Jersey Club was popular.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:20.440
When nobody knew what Jersey club was,

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:21.650
when they were in their dorm rooms,

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:23.663
and it had no title to the genre,

00:11:23.663 --> 00:11:25.411
they were playing patty-cake.

00:11:25.411 --> 00:11:28.120
They were playing footwork, percolator,

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:30.160
all these things because it made you move.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:31.080
It was something to dance to,

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:32.560
and dance is universal.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:35.610
- The reason why people
respond that way is because

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:39.740
traditionally in Africa, it's
a call to call and response.

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:42.510
So when the person bangs the drum,

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:43.760
the person dances.

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:45.780
The purpose of us dancing back then,

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:48.950
in those times were to
call upon our ancestors.

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:50.679
So the call is the,

00:11:50.679 --> 00:11:52.709
(patting)

00:11:52.709 --> 00:11:55.080
and the response is the dance.

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:57.220
- As the dance culture
surrounding Jersey clubs

00:11:57.220 --> 00:11:58.800
spread throughout the state,

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.500
new DJs began to emerge
with their dance crews

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:03.890
known as street teams.

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:06.037
The dance moves that
premiered alongside the music

00:12:06.037 --> 00:12:08.110
became the highlight of the parties.

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:09.550
- Like how did you make the running man?

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:10.810
You know what I'm saying?

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:15.100
Like, like, like that's just
so creative and it went viral.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:15.933
You know what I'm saying?

00:12:15.933 --> 00:12:17.390
It was on Fortnite.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:18.605
It was on Ellen.

00:12:18.605 --> 00:12:20.960
You heard it here first.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:24.330
Like that's a whole viral dance.

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:26.879
Like how did you come
up with the running man?

00:12:26.879 --> 00:12:28.627
- It's not like I sat home and you know,

00:12:28.627 --> 00:12:31.010
was like, you go left over right.

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:34.030
Though the terminology that
people use today is labbing.

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:36.298
So when you're labbing,
you know what I'm saying?

00:12:36.298 --> 00:12:37.870
You just start coming up with stuff

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:39.210
and you just start doing stuff.

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:40.780
And it's like a groove.

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:41.613
You know what I'm saying?

00:12:41.613 --> 00:12:44.637
And that groove is what
people latched on to.

00:12:44.637 --> 00:12:45.705
You know what I'm saying?

00:12:45.705 --> 00:12:48.380
(crowd yelling)

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.190
- Jersey club isn't just music and dance.

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:54.240
It's energy, life, and community,

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:57.040
creating a safe space for the
young people of New Jersey.

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:00.710
- It takes the kids off
the streets in my opinion.

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:02.743
It saves a lot of people.

00:13:02.743 --> 00:13:04.780
There's a lot of gang violence
where we all come from.

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:06.862
Irvington, Orange, East Orange, Newark,

00:13:06.862 --> 00:13:09.820
a bunch of gang violence,
a lot of broken families.

00:13:09.820 --> 00:13:10.653
So kids try to,

00:13:10.653 --> 00:13:13.440
it's like an outlet for a
lot of us is like, love.

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:14.610
We get love from everybody.

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:16.700
And everybody's so in tune with it.

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:18.840
So it saves, it literally saves everybody.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:20.250
It brings happiness.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:25.250
- I started off as a rough
kid, went gang banger.

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:27.550
I did basketball, gang
bangin' and basketball

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.430
was my only thing, my option.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:31.570
Dancing kind of saved me

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.170
and I've been through real life stuff.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:35.580
So my way to cope was dancing.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:37.940
It became more spiritual,

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:40.763
11 years later I'm here still doing it.

00:13:40.763 --> 00:13:43.430
(crowd yelling)

00:13:47.380 --> 00:13:49.050
- These days, it's easy to see

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:51.110
how the women of Jersey club are helping

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:53.750
to push the genre into the future.

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:55.380
But back in the early 2000s,

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:58.180
there was a legendary DJ
who laid the groundwork

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:00.830
by lifting club music to new heights.

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:03.710
Can you tell us how much
K-Swift means to you?

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:06.297
- Rest in peace K-Swift,
Baltimore club queen,

00:14:06.297 --> 00:14:08.450
the club queen period.

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:10.780
K-Swift meant so much to me,

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:14.260
but there was definitely a
big void when she passed away.

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:17.060
- K-Swift was a legendary
DJ from Baltimore.

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:19.090
Her tragic death in 2008

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:21.510
left behind a hole in
the local music scene.

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:26.510
- K-Swift was like the
face for B-more Club.

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:28.510
Wherever she went, you know,

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:30.009
people just followed her,

00:14:30.009 --> 00:14:31.760
and they loved her for what she did.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:33.230
You know, she was phenomenal.

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:35.290
- She was a woman, a black woman

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:40.020
that was carrying the torch to
bringing Baltimore club music

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:43.140
and East coast club music
in general to new heights.

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:45.680
- Her death took a toll
on a lot of people, man.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:48.180
I feel like, you know,
after she passed away

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:49.940
I mean the city as a whole, you know

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:53.180
as far as playing club music,
it kind of just fell apart.

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:55.010
Everybody just kind of lost his steam.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:55.843
Like, you know,

00:14:55.843 --> 00:14:58.600
like that sucked the life
out of everybody man.

00:14:58.600 --> 00:14:59.900
- Me and my home girl,

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:01.640
TT the artist from Baltimore,

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:02.910
she's Baltimore's club queen,

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:04.340
and I'm Jersey club queen.

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:07.470
We doing our best to try to represent

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:09.260
K-Swift and a bright light and,

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:11.860
you know finish the work
that she started real tall.

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:17.619
Just as K-Swift's legacy
inspired the musicians of today,

00:15:17.619 --> 00:15:21.730
today's artists are already
inspiring the next generation.

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:25.590
- When I first saw Maji,
like I was so in awe

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:28.300
I was like, look at this
young girl killing it.

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:30.060
I never saw that in my whole life.

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:32.170
After that, I just, you
know, made it my business.

00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:34.916
Like I have to work with this young girl.

00:15:34.916 --> 00:15:36.528
(upbeat music)

